      <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="quick-answer">Quick answer</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p style="margin:0 0 0.5rem"><strong>How many new spells are in Enshrouded 1.0?</strong> Nine.</p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 0.5rem"><strong>Which ones?</strong> Mana Drain, Entangle, Pillar of Flames, Blizzard, Ball Lightning, Summon Fire Totem, Summon Ice Totem, Summon Shock Totem and Summon Hollow Skeleton.</p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 0.5rem"><strong>Biggest addition?</strong> Summons — the game's first spell category for permanent companions (tougher skeletons and totems).</p>
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>Also new:</strong> 22 tier-7 Eternal spell versions, a piercing heavy attack for wands, and two skill-tree nodes that raise your summon limits.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="new-spells">The 9 new spells</h2>
  <p>
    Keen Games has focused 1.0 on the mage playstyle, which had the least variety. The nine new spells
    spread across crowd control, area damage and the new summons category:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Spell</th><th>Type</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Mana Drain</td><td>Sustain</td><td>Siphons a fixed amount of mana per second from an enemy and adds it directly to your mana pool.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Entangle</td><td>Crowd control</td><td>Roots enemies in place with vines, with area coverage — useful for holding a group while you reposition.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pillar of Flames</td><td>Direct damage</td><td>Straightforward fire damage spell, one of the new instant offensive options.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Blizzard</td><td>Area of effect</td><td>Ice area spell with a damage-over-time effect that slows or freezes enemies caught inside.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ball Lightning</td><td>Area of effect</td><td>Another area option for groups, giving mages a second burst pick alongside Blizzard.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Summon Fire Totem</td><td>Summon</td><td>Places a fire totem that fights alongside you.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Summon Ice Totem</td><td>Summon</td><td>Ice totem summon for elemental variety in a totem build.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Summon Shock Totem</td><td>Summon</td><td>Shock totem summon that completes the elemental totem set.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Summon Hollow Skeleton</td><td>Summon</td><td>Calls a skeleton into battle with a staff — the base of the new necromancy-style build.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p class="muted">
    Spell descriptions reflect Keen Games' official pre-launch notes. Exact damage, mana cost and
    duration numbers are published in the launch changelog; we update this page when they are confirmed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="summons">New category: summons</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      Before 1.0 there were no summons in Enshrouded. The full release adds a dedicated summons
      category with two directions:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Totems</strong> — fire, ice and shock versions that act as stationary or semi-stationary allies.</li>
      <li><strong>Hollow Skeleton</strong> — a staff-cast skeleton, closer to a classic necromancer pet.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      Two new skill-tree nodes exist specifically to raise how many you can field at once:
      <strong>Advanced Necromancy</strong> (more skeletons) and <strong>Totem Mastery</strong> (more totems).
      If you plan to play a summoner, those two nodes matter more than raw damage passives, because they
      increase your summon count rather than improving the summons you already have.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="eternal">Eternal spells (tier 7, 22 versions)</h2>
  <p>
    Eternal spells are the versions that <strong>do not consume spell ammo</strong>, so you cast them
    indefinitely. 1.0 expands them in three ways:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Change</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Volume</td><td>22 tier-7 Eternal spell versions are added across the spell list.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Upgrades</td><td>Eternal spells can now be upgraded like weapons and armour, with their own perks.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Example perk</td><td>Reduced mana cost, which stacks directly onto the rebalance below.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Archers get the same treatment: <strong>all Eternal arrows are now upgradeable</strong>, and three
    new ones arrive — <strong>Eternal Fire</strong>, <strong>Eternal Shock</strong> and
    <strong>Eternal Bone</strong> arrows.
  </p>

  <h2 id="wands">Wand changes</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Change</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Heavy attack</td><td>Wand heavy attacks pierce through enemies instead of stopping at the first target, and can interrupt weaker enemies mid-attack.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Aerial Wand Attack</td><td>New mid-air attack with area damage.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Wand Evasion Attack</td><td>A wider piercing projectile paired with a backward hop, adding mobility while dodging.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Targeting</td><td>Soft and hard lock now prioritise the centre of the screen and nearby enemies, with separate settings for ranged and melee.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Explosive barrels</td><td>Barrels can now be targeted with wands.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Wand/staff switching</td><td>Switching between wands and staves received fixes.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="skills">New mage and archer skills</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Skill</th><th>Class</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Advanced Necromancy</td><td>Mage</td><td>Increases your skeleton summon limit.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Totem Mastery</td><td>Mage</td><td>Increases your totem summon limit.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Blast and Cast</td><td>Mage</td><td>After a wand critical hit, boosts your next staff attack's base damage by 50% within 10 seconds.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Aerial Wand Attack</td><td>Mage</td><td>Unlocks the mid-air area wand attack.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Wand Evasion Attack</td><td>Mage</td><td>Unlocks the piercing evasion attack.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Puncture</td><td>Archer</td><td>More ranged piercing damage.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Barrage</td><td>Archer</td><td>Chance to restore bow durability on hits.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Survival Instinct</td><td>Archer</td><td>Killing Shroud-infested enemies with a bow or dagger adds time in the Shroud.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="muted">
    Because skills were added and moved, <strong>your skill tree resets at 1.0</strong>. Re-spend your
    points before you leave your base on launch day — see the
    <a href="/guides/enshrouded-1-0-skill-tree-reset/">skill tree reset guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rebalance">Mana cost and cast time rebalance</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      Mana costs and casting times were tuned against each spell's actual damage output and usability.
      In practice: heavy, powerful spells stay slow, and quick spells stay fast for everyday use. Enemy
      elemental weaknesses were reworked at the same time, so spells that felt weak against a specific
      faction in Early Access may behave differently after 1.0.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="build">What to do on launch day</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Re-assign your skill points first.</strong> The tree resets, and the new summon nodes change what is worth taking.</li>
    <li><strong>Check your ranged aim assist setting.</strong> New aim assist options are on by default for controllers and off for mouse and keyboard.</li>
    <li><strong>Test one summon.</strong> Totems and Hollow Skeleton behave differently from direct-damage spells; a quick test tells you whether a summoner build suits you before you commit points.</li>
    <li><strong>Look at Eternal versions of spells you already use.</strong> Since Eternal spells skip ammo and can now be upgraded, they are usually the better long-term investment.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p><strong>How many new spells does Enshrouded 1.0 add?</strong><br />Nine: Mana Drain, Entangle, Pillar of Flames, Blizzard, Ball Lightning, Summon Fire Totem, Summon Ice Totem, Summon Shock Totem and Summon Hollow Skeleton.</p>
    <p><strong>Are there summons in Enshrouded now?</strong><br />Yes. Summons are a new spell category in 1.0, covering three elemental totems and a hollow skeleton, with two skill nodes that raise your summon limits.</p>
    <p><strong>Do Eternal spells use ammo?</strong><br />No. Eternal spells do not consume spell ammo, which is why they are the versions to upgrade.</p>
    <p><strong>Will my build still work?</strong><br />Your skill tree is reset because skills were added and moved. The points are refunded, but you have to re-spend them.</p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>When does this arrive?</strong><br />Enshrouded 1.0 leaves Early Access on October 15, 2026 for PC and PlayStation 5.</p>
  </div>

